{
  "propositions": [
    {
      "id": "P1",
      "proposition": "The replacement of § 49-6-3802 defines a \"classroom library,\" unless the context otherwise requires, as materials donated to, purchased by, or acquired by an individual teacher and made available in that teacher's classroom to students assigned to the teacher's class or classes.",
      "importance": "core",
      "section": "SECTION 1",
      "source_quote": "\"Classroom library\" means materials donated to, purchased by, or"
    },
    {
      "id": "P2",
      "proposition": "The replacement of § 49-6-3802 defines a \"library collection,\" unless the context otherwise requires, as materials made available to students by an LEA-operated school or public charter school, excluding materials provided as part of a course curriculum or classroom library.",
      "importance": "core",
      "section": "SECTION 1",
      "source_quote": "materials made available to students as part of a classroom library;"
    },
    {
      "id": "P3",
      "proposition": "The replacement of § 49-6-3802 defines \"materials,\" unless the context otherwise requires, to include books, periodicals, newspapers, manuscripts, films, prints, documents, microfilm, discs, cassettes, videotapes, video games, applications, and subscription content in any form.",
      "importance": "supporting",
      "section": "SECTION 1",
      "source_quote": "applications, and subscription content in any form."
    },
    {
      "id": "P4",
      "proposition": "The bill adds classroom-library references alongside library-collection references in § 49-6-3803: subsection (a) adds \"or classroom library\" after \"a library collection\"; subsection (f) adds \"or in a classroom library\" after \"in the school's library collection\" and \"or classroom library\" after \"from the library collection\"; and subsection (g) adds \"or a classroom library\" after \"a school's library collection\" and \"or classroom library\" after \"a library collection.\"",
      "importance": "core",
      "section": "SECTIONS 2, 5, AND 6",
      "source_quote": "the language \"or classroom library\" immediately after the language \"a library collection\"."
    },
    {
      "id": "P5",
      "proposition": "The bill deletes § 49-6-3803(b).",
      "importance": "core",
      "section": "SECTION 3",
      "source_quote": "subsection (b) and by deleting the first sentence of subsection (d)"
    },
    {
      "id": "P6",
      "proposition": "The bill replaces the first sentence of § 49-6-3803(d) with a requirement that each local board of education and public charter school governing body adopt a policy for developing and reviewing school library collections and for classroom libraries.",
      "importance": "core",
      "section": "SECTION 3",
      "source_quote": "Each local board of education and public charter school governing body shall"
    },
    {
      "id": "P7",
      "proposition": "The policy under § 49-6-3803(d) must include a procedure allowing teachers, or parents and legal guardians of students with access to a classroom library, to review and refer materials in a teacher's classroom library for review and evaluation consistent with § 49-6-3803.",
      "importance": "core",
      "section": "SECTION 4",
      "source_quote": "library for review and evaluation consistent with this section;"
    },
    {
      "id": "P8",
      "proposition": "The policy under § 49-6-3803(d) must include a procedure ensuring that parents and legal guardians of students with access to a classroom library have multiple opportunities throughout the school year to view the books in their student's classroom libraries.",
      "importance": "core",
      "section": "SECTION 4",
      "source_quote": "access to a classroom library have multiple opportunities throughout the school year to"
    },
    {
      "id": "P9",
      "proposition": "The policy under § 49-6-3803(d) must prohibit teachers from knowingly or intentionally circumventing the LEA's or public charter school's library collection policy through their classroom libraries. Examples include materials found by the local board of education, public charter school governing body, or state textbook and instructional materials quality commission pursuant to § 49-6-2201(m) to be inappropriate for the age and maturity levels of students who may access them, or materials found by the local board or governing body to be unsuitable for, or inconsistent with, the school's educational mission.",
      "importance": "core",
      "section": "SECTION 4",
      "source_quote": "A provision prohibiting teachers from knowingly or intentionally circumventing"
    },
    {
      "id": "P10",
      "proposition": "The act takes effect upon becoming a law.",
      "importance": "supporting",
      "section": "SECTION 7",
      "source_quote": "This act takes effect upon becoming a law, the public welfare requiring it."
    }
  ]
}
